\details{
\subsection{Subrounds and rotation groups}{

\code{panels} takes three forms. A count, \code{panels = 12}, makes 12 rotation
groups whose occasions a schedule or \code{\link[=rotation_program]{rotation_program()}} can declare
later. A schedule makes rotation groups and declares their occasions. In
both, a unit selected with certainty is kept apart from the rotating
units and is active at every occasion. \code{panels = subrounds(12)} makes 12
subrounds instead. A unit belongs to one subround only, whether or not it
was selected with certainty, so it is visited once.

The assignment is the one \link{panel_assignment} describes, randomized with
fixed quotas within each stratum along the frame order, so every
subround spreads over the stratum like the whole sample. The subrounds
are recorded in the \code{.panel} column.
}

\subsection{Equal subrounds}{

A stratum gets the same number of units in every subround when its
sample size is a multiple of \code{k}. Survey programs that publish estimates
by month allocate in such multiples, for example 24 EAs per district over
12 months. Otherwise the counts differ by one between subrounds. A
stratum with fewer than \code{k} units is refused, since some subround would
hold none of its units and could not estimate it.
}

\subsection{Weights}{

The full-sample \code{.weight} is valid for the combined sample, which is
what \code{execute(sample, period = 1)} returns. A subround's own weights come
from \code{execute(sample, period = 1, subround = s)}, which multiplies
\code{.weight} by the exact inverse of the subround's activation chance, the
block's quota for that subround over the block size. That factor is \code{k}
when a stratum's units fill whole blocks evenly, and it differs from \code{k}
otherwise, so multiplying by \code{k} by hand is not generally valid.
\link[=summary.tbl_sample]{summary()} reports how evenly each stratum fills the
subrounds.

A subround exports to survey as one phase by ultimate clusters, whatever
method drew the sample, EAs drawn by size included. \code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}}
describes the design. A subround of a sample drawn with equal
probabilities also has a two-phase export, the activation as its second
phase, with \code{as_svydesign(method = "full")}.
}

\subsection{Subrounds in a multistage design}{

Subrounds assigned at the first stage of a multistage design give the
field teams their work plan. \code{dplyr::filter(sample, .panel == s)} lists
the primary units of subround \code{s}, which is what the listing teams need.
That table is a work list, not a sample to continue. Its weights describe
the whole period without the subround's activation factor, and
\code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}} refuses a stage drawn from it
(\code{samplyr_error_modified_sample}).

The later stages are drawn from the sample itself, either for one
subround with that subround's listing,
\code{sample <- execute(sample, listing, period = 1, subround = s)}, or for
every subround at once, \code{execute(sample, listing)}. The section "Drawing
the later stages occasion by occasion" of \code{\link[=execute]{execute()}} states the rules of the
first form. \code{execute(sample, period = 1, subround = s)} gives a
subround's sample and weights once its later stages are drawn.
}
